---
functor: 1
title: Fences and wrap
---

set heading(numbering: none)

Brackets are operations. A fence wraps a group or a symbol, and grows with the content.

| Fence | Example | Result |
|:--|:--|:-:|
| `paren` | `(a + b).paren` | $(a + b).paren$ |
| `brack` | `(a + b).brack` | $(a + b).brack$ |
| `brace` | `(a + b).brace` | $(a + b).brace$ |
| `angle` | `(a + b).angle` | $(a + b).angle$ |
| `vbar` | `(x).vbar` | $(x).vbar$ |
| `floor` | `(x).floor` | $(x).floor$ |
| `ceil` | `(x).ceil` | $(x).ceil$ |

# Double and one-sided

Write `x2` after a fence to double it. Write `left` or `right` after a fence to keep one side.

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `(v).vbar.x2` | $(v).vbar.x2$ |
| `(M).brack.x2` | $(M).brack.x2$ |
| `(x).brace.left` | $(x).brace.left$ |
| `(x).angle.right` | $(x).angle.right$ |

# Meaning names

These calls give the meaning, and a rule gives the fence.

| Call | Default fence | Example | Result |
|:--|:--|:--|:-:|
| `abs(x)` | `vbar` | `abs(x - y)` | $abs(x - y)$ |
| `norm(v)` | `vbar.x2` | `norm(v)` | $norm(v)$ |
| `inner(u, v)` | `angle` | `inner(u, v)` | $inner(u, v)$ |
| `floor(x)` | `floor` | `floor(frac(n, 2))` | $floor(frac(n, 2))$ |
| `ceil(x)` | `ceil` | `ceil(x)` | $ceil(x)$ |

```fn
set norm(fence: vbar)
set abs(grow: magnify)
set inner(fence: paren)
set floor(fence: brack)
set ceil(fence: brack)

$norm(v) + abs(frac(a, b)) + inner(u, v) + floor(x) + ceil(x)$
```

# wrap: any symbol as a fence

`wrap(glyph, body)` puts the glyph at the left and the glyph turned left to right at the right. `wrap(left, right, body)` gives two different sides.

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `wrap(L.script, x + y)` | $wrap(L.script, x + y)$ |
| `wrap(T.frak, S.frak, a)` | $wrap(T.frak, S.frak, a)$ |

A `sym` can name a fence, and a rule can use it:

```fn
sym lsf = L.script
set vec(fence: lsf)

$vec(1, 2)$
```

# Grow: stretch or magnify

| Value | Result | Good for |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `stretch` | the straight parts grow, the ends keep their shape | brackets and bars |
| `magnify` | the whole glyph grows | letters and symbols that must keep their shape |

`set wrap(grow: magnify)` sets it for `wrap`. Each kind also has a `grow` option, for example `set vec(grow: magnify)`. `stretch` uses `deform`: the straight parts grow, and the ends and thin strokes keep their shape. The `tall` operation does the same on one symbol, for example `integral.tall`.

# Fence values in rules

A `fence` value in a rule is a fence operation (`brack`, `vbar.x2`, `brace.left`), the word `none`, or any one symbol, for example `L.script` or a `sym`.
